    "text_frames": [
      {
        "id": "notes",
        "position": [
          2,
          1
        ],
        "params": {
          "text": "**CT05 \u2014 Why does a clipped actuator keep pushing after release?**  \n**Predict \u2192 Run \u2192 Inspect \u2192 Change one thing.** Run 12 s at 100 Hz. Reference 0 \u2192 1.5 at 1 s \u2192 0.4 at 4 s; actuator limited to \u00b11.  \nThe 1.5 command is unreachable. Compare actual control, unlimited demand and integral state; free has no anti-windup, aw uses 6/s back-calculation.  \nDouble-click aw; select i_rate, F4: weights [1,6] \u2192 [1,0]. Return to the root tab, save/reopen/rerun. Both paths should now match.  \nSource/state index n is observed at Scope time (n+1)/100 s. States update afterward; no extra feedback delay is needed.  \n[Open CT05 student lab](qrc:/s7studio/help/education/labs/CT05-actuator-windup/README.md) \u00b7 [MIT PID lecture](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-06-principles-of-automatic-control-fall-2012/resources/mit16_06f12_lecture_10/) \u00b7 [MIT windup notes](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-017j-design-of-electromechanical-robotic-systems-fall-2009/MIT2_017JF09_feedback.pdf)",
          "text_format": "markdown",
          "width": 1800,
          "height": 400,
          "font_size": 20
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
